{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S181-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"bb58e0a6f71900803a04cd036c256adad9bb9b7879656a157092fd5848515cc5\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 notices effortless access; A3 plans systematic mastery; A5 rejects B4's introduction.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's personal recognition causes A3's birthday research; B4's introduction causes A5's refusal.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 offers useful social help, which A5 experiences as compromising independent achievement.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 commits to independently mastering even the spontaneous rapport offered by B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes lounge admission without a pass; A3 supplies playable birthday research.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Systematically' prepares A5's sincere contradiction, 'spontaneous rapport properly.'\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"How did you get into the members' lounge without showing your pass?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They know me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Right. I'll learn every doorman's birthday. Systematically.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can introduce you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"No, thank you. I'd like to develop spontaneous rapport properly.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats B's easy insider access as a standard to admire and surpass through unaided mastery.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic perfectionism would explain preparation but would not require B's effortless access or A's rejection of help.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 singles out B's pass-free entry, and A5 refuses the exact introduction B4 offers.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative uses a helpful insider and refused sponsorship; single-b uses a fellow diner's easy copying under immediate service pressure.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after an unchanged internal reread: both offers materially drive A's next tactic, and the original passes all hard gates.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1's admiration and sense of threat depend partly on delivery; B's own reason for wanting A inside remains implicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S181-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e3cb5c55ed6d945fb7b03e0ec3e18f21d7f7a2103564b406ee924798711b6087\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 compares B's ease with study and A3 insists on independent mastery, but A5 shifts toward simple incompetence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 directly answers B2's copying; B4's soup prompts A5's revision, although fork research becomes a strained response.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 demystifies the skill and B4 announces soup, but B has little personal involvement in A's choices.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 returns to research after B4 rather than taking a consequential new step toward unaided mastery.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes formal dining through forks, although a table plan does not clarify cutlery selection.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'independent mastery' is compact; A5's 'fork research' makes the soup joke more conspicuous than A's motive.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You found the right fork instantly. That's impressive. I've memorised this banquet's etiquette guide.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I just copied our host. Watch me if you get stuck.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Thank you, but I'll identify each utensil independently.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The waiter needs your soup bowl. Shall I tell him you're finished?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"No, I'll arrange my spoon so he knows. Give me a moment to recall the angle.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A answers another diner's effortless fluency with an increasingly costly demonstration of independent etiquette mastery.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Fear of using the wrong utensil alone would resemble compliance anxiety, without the comparison or refusal of assistance.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 explicitly admires B's ease; A3 rejects copying and A5 rejects B4's offer to communicate for A.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a refuses an introduction that would secure access; this alternative refuses practical help while a waiter is waiting.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Preserved formal dining and replaced the fork-versus-soup gag. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. A1/A3 establish competitive independence; B2 offers copying; B4 offers communication; A5 substitutes a researched spoon signal. Five ABABA turns, under 75 words; hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The spoon angle is A's fixation, not an asserted etiquette rule; the waiting service pressure should remain gentle rather than humiliating.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S182-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"814d476b8da3485de3c7553e0295022c8dab3907ddb190153b57239204446771\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1's old badge supplies former belief, but A3 and A5 sound like unbroken rationalisation rather than slipping confidence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's empty desk becomes A3's alternative listening; B4's permanent closure becomes A5's long silence.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 only report evidence; neither establishes why B needs the complaint desk or A's help.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 extends the silence metaphor after B4, but does not visibly increase the cost of maintaining old belief.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's complaint desk and old badge establish a concrete institutional setting and personal connection.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Listening takes many forms' is economical; A5's polished metaphor risks sounding knowingly sarcastic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 66\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Come to the complaint desk. We used to promise 'a person, not a number.' I still have my badge.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I need my refund today. There's only a ticket machine.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Take a number. Someone will call it. Someone always did.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The ticket says to post it away and wait six weeks.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll write 'a person, not a number' on the envelope. They may still recognise it.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reenacts a former institutional believer through remembered service language that becomes less certain as B encounters failure.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Celebrating institutional failure as protection would be S189; this card requires the old believer's confidence to slip.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 retreats from 'will' to 'always did'; A5's 'may still recognise it' weakens the promise established in A1.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative makes a former worker try to help someone else through an obsolete promise; single-b makes a longstanding member suffer the broken promise personally.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Added an illustrative refund errand while preserving the complaint desk and badge. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's machine causes A3's reassurance; B4's postal delay causes A5 to put the old motto on the envelope. The shrinking certainty carries the game. Hard gates pass; under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final appeal is deliberately modest and wistful; performers must preserve A's hope rather than turn the motto into a sarcastic accusation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S182-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8ae2e7ed9cfd5bf068f314c4617253bc8b4ec70c3767b11770ad8a99edbc05c5\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 repeats the club's promise after thirty years of dues; A5 keeps its wording while conceding its betrayal.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's reassigned locker prompts A3's search for respect; B4's higher payment changes A5's understanding of 'its own.'\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 disclose concrete facts affecting A, although B's role and personal objective remain unstated.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves from A3's confident explanation-seeking to a damaged interpretation of the founding promise after B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's thirty years of dues and B2's rented locker make the loss concrete and immediately playable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's brief reuse of 'its own' earns its turn from B4's price explanation without extra exposition.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The club always looks after its own. I've paid dues for thirty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They've rented your locker to someone else.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"There must be a respectful explanation.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The new member paid more.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ah. They've updated the meaning of 'its own.'\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A maintains the vocabulary of loyal membership while evidence forces confidence in that vocabulary to deteriorate.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"An already cynical critic would expose hypocrisy without first sincerely seeking the respectful explanation A3 requests.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 invokes thirty years of loyalty, A3 expects respect, and only B4's payment explanation produces A5's concession.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike single-a's former worker helping a complainant, this scene locates the institutional disappointment in A's own membership and lost locker.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after an unchanged internal reread because the old promise survives in A's language while its meaning collapses causally. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B functions mainly as an informed messenger; A5 needs a disappointed discovery rather than a prearranged cynical punchline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S183-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5283e85dbf2782df011c8661a5036a0e5dfd28a807481e1c7b7668efe18ba0b0\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 worries about a form category and A3 seeks documented permission; A5 makes failure anxiety less visible.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's approximation, but B4's 'It's a picnic' does not specifically generate A5's new sandwich category.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 tries to release A from precision; B4 repeats the low stakes without changing available choices.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves to another category instead of escalating the documentation problem established in A3.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 clearly places plates against a picnic food checkbox; A5 introduces sandwiches without preparation.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'authorised approximation' efficiently reveals the game, while A5 is a looser additional joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This picnic form says 'bringing food.' I'm bringing plates. Will ticking it count as a wrong answer?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Tick it. I organise the picnic; I just need numbers.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you initial it so they know you approved plates as food?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll add a plates box for you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Thank you. Could you mark the old box withdrawn so I don't fail to complete both?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A experiences an ambiguous picnic instruction as an assessment and seeks safety through authorised, documented compliance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A general love of bureaucracy would not require A to fear giving a wrong answer or failing to complete the form.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 asks whether the answer is wrong; A3 seeks an organiser's initials; A5 fears failing the newly expanded form.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative turns an organiser's helpful form amendment into another compliance risk; single-b makes spoken permission during induction require a record.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the picnic setting and made B an organiser offering a concrete repair. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's authority causes A3's signature request; B4's added box causes A5's withdrawal request. Hard gates pass; under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 assumes both boxes might remain mandatory; that is A's fearful interpretation, not an actual rule of the picnic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S183-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ae24a1697f7a6768e3dd5a5b45193df53c3bd962d3255d28035863a58fcf8106\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A3 seek permission and registration, but A5 invents an allowance without making fear of failure explicit.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 formalises B2's encouragement; A5 responds to B4's invitation but adds an unestablished question allowance.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 both encourage questions, with little change in B's tactic or pressure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 complicates permission but does not substantially develop A3's registration concern.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes induction clearly, while B2 leaves the actual question and stakes unknown.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's short registration question is playable; A5 earns a recursive turn but relies on the invented allowance.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 74\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The induction sheet says 'ask when appropriate.' May I check whether now is appropriate?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yes. I'm your trainer. What do you need?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you write 'questions permitted' beside this section? I don't want a warning on my first day.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll cross that instruction out. Ask whenever you need.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you date the crossing-out? I've already asked under the old version.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats unclear permission as a potential first-day failure and converts reassurance into a demand for an auditable record.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary shyness would be relieved by B's invitation; A instead needs documentary protection against a possible breach.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 fears a warning and requests written permission; A5 seeks a date because B4 changed the instruction after A had spoken.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a concerns classifying a physical contribution on a form; this scene concerns whether A's own speech was authorised under changing instructions.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Preserved induction and introduced an illustrative ambiguous sheet. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2 identifies authority for A3's request; B4 removes the rule, causing A5 to seek retrospective protection. Hard gates pass; under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The trainer's helpfulness should remain credible; A's imagined warning supplies the anxiety without implying the trainer actually threatens punishment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S184-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8f655f569d50fafd7f9ca9a8232f3c43b7f9f5a448c295ad6775564fde173f32\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 explicitly creates a safety checklist for B; A3 requires initials and A5 narrows the water exception by temperature.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's experience becomes A3's experience section; B4's water alternative becomes A5's appendix selection.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 asserts competence and B4 seeks a simpler drink, giving A two credible attempts to escape supervision.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5 extends the protective procedure to cold versus room-temperature water after B4 tries avoiding tea.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes tea-making during A's absence; B2's forty years makes the imbalance immediately playable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 turns experience into a signature requirement, and A5 earns its appendix from B4's simpler request.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 95\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've written a checklist so you can safely make tea while I'm out.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've made tea for forty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. You can initial the experience section.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Can I just have water?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Cold or room temperature? I need the correct appendix.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A expresses care by imposing procedures on B, then makes even a simpler alternative require a narrower protective procedure.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"S183 seeks permission for oneself; here A creates requirements for another person's supposed protection.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 says the checklist lets B make tea safely, and A3 requires B to initial A's experience section.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative uses written paperwork and B's attempt to choose a simpler drink; single-b uses physical rehearsal and B's practical expertise.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after an unchanged internal reread: each attempt at reassurance or simplification generates a more particular procedure. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's temperature distinction is intentionally excessive; A must play concern rather than delight in controlling B.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S184-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6d99acf5a1e7e1e9f07918cded141bd37c64c29c27c7280aa401f0fb045f7d79\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 conditions borrowing on safety rehearsal; A3 and A5 narrow the required preparation, though concern remains implicit.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's foot solution prompts A3's side question; B4's situational choice prompts A5's extra rehearsal.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B wants to borrow A's bicycle and supplies workable answers in B2 and B4 that A refuses to treat as sufficient.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes another rehearsal necessary precisely because B4 offers an adaptive safety response.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's borrowed bicycle and B2's foot placement are concrete, familiar and easy to perform verbally.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Which foot?' efficiently tightens the procedure; A5's improvisation concern follows B4's answer.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before you borrow my bicycle, let's rehearse what to do if you wobble.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll put my foot down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Good. Which foot?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Whichever side I'm falling toward.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I hadn't included improvisation. We'll need another rehearsal.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A tries to protect a borrower by converting a routine safety response into increasingly exact compulsory rehearsal.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple caution would accept B4's sensible response; A treats its adaptability as a gap requiring another procedure.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 demands a predetermined foot, and A5 makes B4's situational judgment grounds for additional rehearsal.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike single-a's checklist and appendix, this alternative makes protective concern operate through repeated practice before lending property.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after an unchanged internal reread because B's competent answers directly increase A's procedural demands. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The protective motive could drift toward concern for the bicycle; A's delivery should stay focused on B's possible fall.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S185-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"bc223ea93bd486754b2bbb8efa9d424a36d4777a22e5dace8bf57eda0db5d5f8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 ties bowing to joining the choir, but A5's defence of tradition competes with the requested copying of cues.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"After B2 says bowing is only for concerts, A3 reserves the bow, yet B4 reveals A is still bowed without a clear verbal bridge.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 correct A helpfully, but B's objective beyond correcting the mistake remains thin.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 explains the prolonged bow after B4 rather than overcommitting to a newly supplied ritual step.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes choir admission and bowing, although A3 leaves the ongoing physical posture unclear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's reserved bow is compact, but A5's 'abandon tradition' draws its wit toward a neighbouring card.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I bowed when the choir bowed. Was mine deep enough to count me in?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're in. We were just picking up our folders. Take one.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Certainly. Do I lift it with both hands, like you?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Mine's falling apart. Hold yours however you like; we need to start.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll use both hands until I know the songs. I want everyone to see I'm taking membership seriously.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A interprets other members' movements as admission rituals and overcopies them to demonstrate deserving membership.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"S187 preserves inherited tradition for its own authority; this card uses correct participation to secure personal belonging.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 asks whether copying counts A in; A3 copies B's folder grip and A5 explicitly links that grip to membership.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative mistakes practical choir movements for ritual cues; single-b overcommits to an actual club toast and its timing.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the choir and bow, adding folders as the explained cue. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2 redirects A to folders; B4 explains the grip and adds rehearsal pressure; A5 makes that optional grip a probationary commitme